    </nav>

    <h2 id="sec-1">Why the food-hopping pattern changes the inspection</h2>
    <p>The typical tourist in Hanoi rides a few long routes in a week. The typical food-focused traveler rides fifteen short ones. Short hops mean the machine is started cold many times a day, parked and unparked constantly, loaded and unloaded with bags, and maneuvered on crowded pavements outside eateries where it will be nudged, shuffled by parking attendants and leaned against by strangers. Every one of those events is minor; their sum is not, and the pre-ride inspection is how a rider keeps the sum visible instead of discovering it all at once on a busy street.</p>
    <p>Frequent short trips also distort the rider's own perception. On a long ride, a developing fault announces itself in the machine's behavior over an hour of attention. On a ten-minute hop between eateries, the same fault hides behind distractions: the navigation, the hunger, the anticipation of the next bowl. The countermeasure is ritual. An inspection performed the same way at the same time every day, checking the same short list in the same order, is the only reliable way to notice that the rear brake started feeling spongy on Tuesday when every individual ride felt fine.</p>
    <p>The third effect is specific to this audience: cargo. A food-focused day involves bags, boxes and loose items riding in the footwell or on a rack, and cargo interacts with the machine, blocking levers, shifting weight, resting against the exhaust. Part of the pre-ride inspection for this audience is a load check, and this chapter folds it into the routine so that the banh mi gets home in the same condition it left the shop in.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-2">Pickup day: the full inspection with photos</h2>
    <p>The most important inspection of the rental happens once, on pickup day, before the paperwork is signed. Work through the machine from front to back, and take photographs as you go. The photos serve two purposes: they document the machine's existing condition so that any later disagreement about a scratch or a scuff has evidence instead of memory, and they slow you down enough to actually look. Photograph the whole machine from four angles, then close-ups of any existing marks on the panels, the mirrors, the levers and the exhaust cover. The <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/motorbike-rental-deposit-hanoi/">deposit guide</a> covers why those four-angle photos are the single best protection for your deposit at the return.</p>
    <p>The pickup inspection also sets the baseline for everything you check later. Squeeze both brake levers and note the feel, how far they travel before biting, and how firm the stop is. Note the fuel level and the arrangement, whether the tank was full, whether the machine is fuel-injected or carbureted, and how the shop wants it returned. Check that the documents you are given, the registration paper, any rental agreement, are physically on the machine or in your possession as agreed, and photograph them too. Anything that is part of the deal belongs in the photographic record of the deal.</p>
    <p>Ask the shop the maintenance questions while you have their attention. How old are the tires, when were the brakes last looked at, and what should you do if something feels wrong mid-rental, call, or bring it in? A shop that rents machines in a city like Hanoi has answered these questions hundreds of times, and their answers are both useful information and a preview of how the return conversation will go. The <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/food-focused-travelers-bike-choice/">bike choice chapter</a> covers choosing the machine; the pickup inspection is where the chosen machine proves it deserved to be chosen.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-3">Tires and wheels</h2>
    <p>Tires are the machine's entire relationship with the road, and they get the first minute of the daily check. Kneel or crouch once per day and look at both tires properly: pressure judged by how they squat under the machine's weight, a visibly sagging sidewall or a rear tire that looks flat-sided means the pressure has dropped and the nearest air pump is the first stop of the food day. In Hanoi, air pumps are common at roadside repair stands and many fuel stations, and the pressure question is solved in two minutes for a trivial fee or none.</p>
    <p>Tread and sidewalls come next. The tread grooves should be visible and uniform; a worn tire with shallow grooves, especially a smooth rear, loses grip in rain and on the painted road markings that cover the city. Look for the foreign objects that a food-hopping machine collects, nails, glass, and the small screws that litter streets near markets, because a screw picked up outside the spice market is better found in the morning inspection than at the moment it finishes its work. Sidewall cuts and bubbles are a stop-now finding: the machine does not ride until the shop has seen it.</p>
    <p>The wheels themselves rotate and stay put, and both facts are checkable in seconds. Lift each end of the machine and spin the wheel, or simply rock the machine and listen: a wheel that wobbles side to side, grinds, or clicks has a bearing or brake problem that no rider should diagnose by riding. Spokes, on machines that have them, should be present and roughly evenly tensioned, a rim with loose or missing spokes is a finding to mention to the shop on the same day. None of this is expert-level work; it is the same attention a cook gives a knife before service, applied to the thing that carries the cook.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-4">Brakes</h2>
    <p>Brakes earn their own section because they are the one system whose failure is not survivable with good luck. The daily brake check is tactile and takes ten seconds. Standing beside the machine, pull the front lever hard and hold it: the lever should stop firm well before it reaches the grip, with no creeping toward the bar. Squeeze the rear brake, lever or pedal depending on the machine, and feel for the same firm bite. Then roll the machine forward a meter and apply each brake separately and feel that each one alone slows the machine decisively.</p>
    <p>Changes matter more than absolutes. A brake that felt firm at pickup and feels spongy on Thursday has a story, air in the line, a leak, a worn pad, and the story needs the shop, not optimism. Listen as well as feel: a rhythmic scraping or a grinding sound when the wheel spins means pads or a dragged component, and the correct response is to stop riding the machine and have it looked at. Brake fluid, on machines with a visible reservoir, should sit between the marks and look tea-colored at worst; black fluid or a level below minimum is a same-day conversation with the shop.</p>
    <p>The food-focused angle on brakes is cargo and speed discipline. A machine loaded with a day's market purchases, boxes in the footwell, a crate on the rack, stops differently from an empty one, and the inspection habit extends into how you load: heavy items low and centered, nothing resting against the levers, the load checked with your hands before the first corner. When the bag of fresh herbs has to ride between your feet, its position is a braking decision as much as a comfort one, and the pre-ride routine treats it that way.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-5">Controls, cables and switches</h2>
    <p>The controls are the rider's interface, and they get a functional sweep on pickup day and a quick feel-check every morning. Throttle: twist it and release; it should snap back crisply on its own, and any stickiness or a slow return is a cable problem that will tire your wrist at best and confuse the machine at worst. Levers and pedals should pivot smoothly without slop, and the lever positions can usually be adjusted to your hand size, ask at the shop if the reach feels wrong, because lever position is comfort on every single ride of the rental.</p>
    <p>Cables are visible along the handlebars and frame, and the check is visual: no frayed strands, no sharp kinks, no cables rubbed shiny by contact with something they should not touch. A fraying cable is a polite warning, it works for days before it fails, and the inspection is how you cash that warning in at the shop rather than on the Long Bien bridge at rush hour. If the machine's steering feels notchy or tight when you rock the handlebars, that is a finding too, and it belongs on the same list.</p>
    <p>Switches and indicators complete the interface. Left and right indicators, high and low beam, the horn: each gets one activation while you watch and listen, front and rear, every day. The horn in Hanoi traffic is not an accessory; it is a spoken word in the street's language, announcing your position to the flow around you, and a dead horn in this city is a genuine deficit. The whole control sweep takes under a minute once it is a routine, which is less time than the queue for the morning's first bowl.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-6">Lights, horn and the visible machine</h2>
    <p>Lights are how the machine speaks to the traffic, and they are checked as a pair every morning: headlight, low and high, tail light and brake light, both indicators front and rear. The brake light check needs a wall or a window behind you, roll up, squeeze, and look for the red reflection; two seconds, and it confirms that the drivers behind you will receive the single most important signal a stopping scooter sends. A bulb that works intermittently is a bulb that is about to not work at all, and replacement is trivial at any repair stand.</p>
    <p>The visible machine is the rest of the walk-around: mirrors clean and set for your posture, adjusted once and then nudged back into place whenever a parking attendant has folded them; the stand folding fully and snapping into its bracket, because a dropped stand at speed is the classic scooter accident; the seat latching properly; the under-seat cavity closing dry and latched. Look underneath once a day for the day's honest answer: a fresh oil spot under the engine, or a drip from anywhere, is the machine telling you something before the machine escalates.</p>
    <p>For a rider whose day is structured around stops, the visible machine includes the visible parking history. Attendants shuffle bikes constantly, so fold the mirrors when parking, and make the mirror-and-stand reset part of the pre-ride check rather than discovering it a corner later. The walk-around before the first ride of the day is also when you confirm the previous evening's parking did not add any new beauty marks, and the pickup-day photos make that comparison a glance instead of a debate.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-7">The thirty-second pre-hop check</h2>
    <p>The daily inspection is the deep version; the pre-hop check is the fast one, performed before every individual ride, and it compresses to a glance, a squeeze and a pat. Glance: the machine sits normally, tires look round, nothing is leaking underneath, the load is seated, mirrors are where you left them. Squeeze: one firm pull on the front brake, one on the rear, feeling for the familiar firmness. Pat: fuel and keys where they should be, helmet strap fastened before the wheels turn, phone and cards in pockets rather than balanced on the dashboard.</p>
    <p>The point of the pre-hop check is not to find major faults, it is to catch the small, fresh ones: the bag strap now looped near the throttle, the carrier bag leaning against the exhaust, the side stand not fully up, the mirror folded by a passing stranger. Short-hop riders mount and dismount dozens of times a day, and each transition is a small chance for one of these to slip through. Thirty seconds, twice learned and then automatic, is the cheapest insurance in the entire food trip.</p>
    <p>Attach the check to a fixed moment so it survives the hunger. The natural anchor is the walk from the eatery door to the machine: that walk is going to happen anyway, and giving it a script costs nothing. By the second day the sequence runs without conscious thought, the same way a regular at a counter knows their order, and the riding day becomes a sequence of small, calm, verified departures instead of a series of assumptions that everything is as it was an hour ago.</p>

    <h2 id="sec-8">When something is not right</h2>
    <p>Every inspection occasionally finds something, and the response is graded, not dramatic. A tire low on pressure: air pump, two minutes, continue. A bulb out: repair stand, ten minutes, a trivial fee, continue. A new scuff on a panel: photograph it the day you notice it, so the timeline is honest for everyone. These are the routine findings of a machine living a busy city life, and the rental exists to be ridden, so the plan is to handle them and carry on with the day's eating.</p>
    <p>The serious findings are short and worth memorizing: brakes that pull toward the bar or feel spongy, a grinding or clicking wheel, a throttle that sticks, a wobble in the steering or wheels, a cut or bubble in a tire, a fresh oil spot that returns after cleaning. Any of these means the machine stops riding until the shop has seen it, and the shop conversation happens by phone or in person the same day, not after the next bowl. The <a href="/faq/">FAQ</a> page covers the general questions about rental support, and the shop's own answers at pickup, which you collected in the section on the first inspection, tell you exactly how they want to be told.</p>
    <p>Keep the evidence habit to the end. The same four-angle photos that protected the deposit at pickup close the rental at return, and the inspection routine's quiet gift is that you arrive at the hand-back knowing the machine's full story, every scuff dated, every fault resolved, every photo in order. A food-focused trip is built on trust in other people's counters and kitchens; the inspection routine extends that same trust to your own machine, and it pays the same dividends, reliably, one bowl at a time.</p>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions about pre-ride inspections for a Hanoi rental</h2>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>What should I check before every ride on my Hanoi rental motorbike?</h2>
      <p>The thirty-second version: a glance at tire shape and the ground under the machine, a squeeze of both brakes for familiar firmness, the load seated, mirrors in place, stand fully up, helmet fastened. Once a day, do the fuller check of tires, brakes, controls, lights, horn and visible condition described in this chapter.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Should I photograph the motorbike at pickup?</h2>
      <p>Yes. Four angles of the whole machine plus close-ups of any existing marks, and photographs of the papers you were given. The photos document the starting condition, protect your deposit conversation at return, and give you a baseline for comparing anything you notice later in the rental.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>How do I check tire pressure without a gauge?</h2>
      <p>Look at the squat: a tire bulging visibly at the contact patch or a flat-sided rear is low. Roadside repair stands and many fuel stations have pumps, and airing up takes a couple of minutes. Also check for embedded nails or glass and look at the tread grooves while you are crouched down there.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>What brake problems mean I should stop riding immediately?</h2>
      <p>A lever that creeps toward the grip, a spongy or fading feel that was firm before, grinding or rhythmic scraping from either wheel, or brake fluid below the minimum mark. Stop riding and contact the shop the same day. Brakes are the one system where waiting is never the right plan.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Does frequent short riding between food stops damage the rental bike?</h2>
      <p>Not if the machine is checked. Short frequent hops stress cold starts, parking shuffles and cargo handling more than the engine, and the daily inspection is designed to catch exactly those effects. Check the machine in the morning, load it deliberately, and the food-hopping pattern is entirely normal use.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="faq-entry">
      <h2>Who fixes small problems like a dead bulb during my rental?</h2>
      <p>Roadside repair stands handle bulbs, air, and small items quickly and cheaply, and they are everywhere in Hanoi. Ask the shop at pickup how they prefer to handle small repairs and what to do about anything serious; their answer is the policy that applies to your rental, and the FAQ page covers the general questions.</p>
    </article>
